<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A school canteen in Embu sells <m>x</m> plates of githeri and <m>y</m> plates of pilau in a day.</p><ul><li>A plate of githeri costs Ksh <m>30</m> to make and a plate of pilau costs Ksh <m>70</m>; the canteen has Ksh <m>6300</m>.</li><li>At least <m>50</m> plates of pilau are sold.</li><li>The canteen sells more than <m>100</m> plates.</li></ul><p>Wambui wrote this model, with <m>x \ge 0</m> and <m>y \ge 0</m>:</p><p>(1) <m>{30x + 70y \le 6300}</m> <nbsp/><nbsp/> (2) <m>{y \le 50}</m> <nbsp/><nbsp/> (3) <m>{x + y \gt 100}</m></p><p>Each inequality (1) to (3) is meant to match the sentence in the same place. One of them does not match its sentence.</p><p>(a) Which one?</p><div>[ None of them: her model is right. | (1) | (2) | (3) ]  </div><p>(b) Write it correctly.</p><div><fillin characters="18" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The correct model is (1) <m>{30x + 70y \le 6300}</m>, (2) <m>{y \ge 50}</m>, (3) <m>{x + y \gt 100}</m>.</p><p>Wambui's inequality (<m>{2}</m>) is <m>{y\leq 50}</m>, which does not match its sentence: it should be <m>{y \ge 50}</m>. Read each sentence for its key words (at most <m>\le</m>, at least <m>\ge</m>, more than <m>\gt</m>, fewer than <m>\lt</m>), check which letter each number belongs to, and check a ‘times as many’ sentence with numbers.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>(2)</p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>y\geq 50</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>